          "citation": "495 Mich. 184, 848 N.W.2d 107 (2014)",
          "court": "Michigan Supreme Court",
          "year": 2014,
          "date_decided": "2014-04-22",
          "holding": "The Court held that the relative-placement preference in MCL 722.954a does not apply to a post-termination guardianship determination under MCL 712A.19c(2), and that the trial court permissibly compared placement options using Child Custody Act best-interest factors.",
          "relevance": "Because guardianship under MCL 712A.19c(2) keeps court oversight in place, COH shapes the permanency option most often used for older youth who will not be adopted.",
          "quote": "we hold that there is no preference for placement with relatives as part of a guardianship determination under MCL 712A.19c(2)",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8001639/in-re-coh/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8001639
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Macomber",
          "citation": "436 Mich. 386 (1990)",
          "court": "Michigan Supreme Court",
          "year": 1990,
          "date_decided": "1990-09-26",
          "holding": "The Court held that chapter XIIA gives the probate court broad authority to enter orders affecting adults, including an order requiring a parent to leave the home, where such orders are necessary for the well-being of a child within the court's jurisdiction.",
          "relevance": "Because the court's power over adults is incidental to its jurisdiction over the child, it ends when that jurisdiction over the child ends.",
          "quote": "we hold that the Legislature has conferred very broad authority to the probate court",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8000356/kent-county-department-of-social-services-v-macomber/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8000356
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Harper",
          "citation": "302 Mich. App. 349, 839 N.W.2d 44 (2013)",
          "court": "Michigan Court of Appeals",
          "year": 2013,
          "date_decided": "2013-08-29",
          "holding": "The Court vacated a trial court order directing DHS to remove a mother's name from the child abuse central registry, holding that MCL 722.627 vests expunction authority exclusively in the department and that the parent must exhaust the administrative process first.",
          "relevance": "A registry listing follows a person into professional licensing long after the child's case closes, which is why the exhaustion route matters.",
          "quote": "The plain language of the statute grants exclusive jurisdiction to DHS to control expunction from the central registry.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8006876/in-re-harper/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8006876
        }
